orthant 2 release

@ 0 5 0 0e6ce169ee79625708555ad2b50f5b0f
+ [capture and separation checking](../../philosophy/capture-checking.md) enabled. Using a
+ stream again after it has been consumed, or letting one escape the scope which owns its source,
+ is a compile error in code which enables both checks. Where replay really is wanted, `memoize`
+ drains the stream once into an [immutable] value, which may then be shared freely. That is the
+ explicit, bounded replacement for a lazy list's implicit caching.

@ 0 2 0 0d1446081cc0ccd40132f4f5240743d3
+ mark may be taken, grabbed from and returned to only within `hold`, which keeps the marked
+ region in the buffer until the block ends. Outside `hold`, the cursor is free to discard what
+ it has already passed:

@ 3 0 0 17aa0e17a924169e036e3c389d3d38c0
+ What a block writes to the two streams can be collected instead of shown. `Stdio.capture`
+ runs a block and gives its result beside the text written to each stream, and it reaches
+ whatever writes to them, including the JVM's own `System.out` and Scala's `println`:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val captured = Stdio.capture:
+   Out.println(t"started")
+   Err.println(t"a warning")
+
+ Out.println(captured.out.trim)
+ Out.println(captured.err.trim)
+ ```
+
+ A terminal shows the two streams as one, in the order they were written, and a program which
+ runs a user's snippet usually wants the same. `Err.divert(Out)` sends what a block writes to
+ standard error to standard output instead, so a capture around it holds one interleaved text:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val merged = Stdio.capture:
+   Err.divert(Out):
+     Out.println(t"one")
+     Err.println(t"two")
+     Out.println(t"three")
+
+ Out.println(merged.out.trim)
+ ```
+
+ `Out.divert(Err)` does the reverse. A diversion lasts for its block, and covers the tasks
+ started within it.
+

